discovery_context:
No typecheck or lint tooling exists in this repo — plain JS + node:test (conventions.md 2026-08-01). scripts/chain/ DOES NOT EXIST YET — you create it. (spec-lint will hard-FAIL the scripts/chain/evidence.mjs entry in files_in_scope for exactly that reason — missing path with a missing parent, spec-lint.mjs:287 — this is a known, accepted false positive, already triaged by the lead; do not treat it as a spec defect.)

PATTERN TO MIRROR — argv-array git discipline, scripts/cmux/dispatch.mjs:500-507:

    function gitOutput(args, cwd) {
      return execFileSync('git', args, { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' }).trim()
    }

    function gitOk(args, cwd) {
      const res = spawnSync('git', args, { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' })
      return res.status === 0
    }

Note the surrounding comment at dispatch.mjs:493-498: 'Every invocation is execFileSync with an argv array (never a shell string) ... unpiped, array-form spawns are the only reliable way to get real exit codes and never hit a shell-interpolation hazard.'

ANTI-PATTERN — DO NOT COPY, scripts/cmux/dispatch.mjs:1917-1924:

    function gitPorcelain(worktreePath) {
      if (!worktreePath || !existsSync(worktreePath)) return null
      try {
        return execFileSync('git', ['status', '--porcelain'], { cwd: worktreePath, encoding: 'utf8' })
      } catch {
        return null
      }
    }

It uses a bare --porcelain (no -z, no -uall, unpinned version) and collapses every failure to null. Its own caller treats null as UNVERIFIABLE, which is correct there; here a swallowed git error must THROW, because a hard_fail check that cannot see the tree must never report clean.

FOR STATUS BYTES: pass `encoding: 'buffer'` (or omit encoding) to execFileSync so you get a Buffer, then `buf.toString('utf8')` ONCE over the whole thing before splitting on '\0'. Splitting the raw Buffer on byte 0 and slicing at fixed offsets is the desync bug.

SYMBOLS YOU CALL BUT DO NOT DEFINE:
- assertValidationCommands(cmds) -> string[] and class RefusalError, both exported from scripts/cmux/resolve.mjs (:346 and :326). Current source, resolve.mjs:346-358:

    export function assertValidationCommands(cmds) {
      if (!Array.isArray(cmds)) {
        throw new Error('assertValidationCommands: cmds must be an array')
      }
      const trimmed = cmds.map((c) => (typeof c === 'string' ? c.trim() : c))
      for (const cmd of trimmed) {
        if (typeof cmd !== 'string' || !CMD_RE.test(cmd) || cmd.includes('..')) {
          throw new RefusalError(cmd)
        }
        assertFirstTokenSafe(cmd)
      }
      return trimmed
    }

  IMPORTANT CORRECTION to what you may have been told: assertFirstTokenSafe (resolve.mjs:339) is module-PRIVATE and is reached only through assertValidationCommands — do not try to import it. CMD_RE lives in scripts/cmux/contract.mjs:58 (`/^[A-Za-z0-9 _./:=@+-]{1,200}$/`), not in resolve.mjs, which imports it. You need neither directly.

  DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE, STATE IT IN A COMMENT: assertValidationCommands refuses the WHOLE SET on the first offender (resolve.mjs:322-325 explains why — a partially-expanded permission-rule set is a silently narrower lane). This file calls it PER COMMAND (`assertValidationCommands([cmd])`) so one unsafe command yields one named refused RunResult instead of silently disabling the whole validation lane. Per-command isolation is LOUDER here, not quieter — that is the reason for the divergence and it belongs in a comment next to the call.

- createHash from node:crypto for sha256. Content-hash choice is already settled: sha256 per baseline-dirty entry, NOT `git write-tree` (smaller, zero side effect, no GIT_INDEX_FILE temp-index juggling; baseline dirty sets are 0-10 entries in practice).
- constants from node:os for signal-number lookup (`constants.signals.SIGKILL === 9`).

TEST FIXTURE IDIOM TO REUSE — test/cmux-dispatch.test.mjs:101-111:

    function makeGitCheckout(dir) {
      const checkout = join(dir, 'checkout')
      mkdirSync(checkout, { recursive: true })
      execFileSync('git', ['init', '-q'], { cwd: checkout })
      execFileSync('git', ['config', 'user.email', 'test@example.com'], { cwd: checkout })
      execFileSync('git', ['config', 'user.name', 'Test'], { cwd: checkout })
      writeFileSync(join(checkout, 'README.md'), '# sample repo\n')
      execFileSync('git', ['add', '.'], { cwd: checkout })
      execFileSync('git', ['commit', '-q', '-m', 'init'], { cwd: checkout })
      return checkout
    }

Roots come from mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'chain-evidence-')). Note that macOS TMPDIR is a symlink (/var -> /private/var) — if you ever compare paths, realpathSync BOTH sides (backend-notes.md 2026-08-02).

WHY -uall AND --no-optional-locks AND --porcelain=v1 (put this reasoning in a comment at the argv site): --no-optional-locks avoids index.lock contention with a concurrently-running coder worker; -uall because the default collapses untracked directories to `dir/`, hiding the filenames inside new directories, and a protected-path/scope check that cannot see filenames inside new dirs FAILS OPEN; --porcelain=v1 is pinned against a future git default change.

GOTCHA — `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` exits 128 on an unborn branch. mkdtemp test fixtures hit this the moment you `git init` without committing. Classify it explicitly as its own error code; never store undefined as anchor_sha.

GOTCHA — huge-output.mjs must write with a bounded loop of buffered writes (e.g. 1000 iterations of a 5KB string) rather than one 5MB string, and must write to BOTH stdout and stderr so the shared-fd interleaving is exercised. slow-command.mjs must hold the event loop (e.g. setTimeout with a 30s handle) so SIGKILL is what ends it.

ASSUMPTIONS STATED: (1) touched paths are repo-relative POSIX strings throughout — git emits them that way and gates.mjs compares them against a spec's files_in_scope, which is also repo-relative. Do not absolutize. (2) A baseline's `reason` is caller-supplied free text and is never interpolated into markdown by this file (the sanitization rule of backend-notes.md 2026-08-02 applies to whoever renders it, not here) — but keep it out of any thrown message. (3) The default GATE_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS export is 600000 to match this repo's harness Bash ceiling discussion (A0/#24 set --max-block-s 570 against a 600000ms ceiling); export it frozen so gates.mjs never invents its own.

interface_contract:
Backend (this task) OWNS these shapes; be-28-03's gates.mjs consumes them and must not redefine them.

Baseline = { anchor_sha: string, reason: string, captured_at: string /* ISO */, dirty: { [path: string]: { xy: string, sha256: string|null } } }

Touched = { paths: string[] /* sorted, deduped, repo-relative POSIX */, sources: { committed: string[], dirty: string[], reverted: string[] } }

PorcelainEntry = { xy: string, path: string, from?: string /* present only for R/C */ }

RunResult = { cmd: string, argv: string[], code: number|null, classified: 'exit'|'timeout'|'missing_binary'|'signal'|'signal_unknown'|'refused'|'spawn_error', signal: string|null, elapsed_ms: number, ok: boolean /* code === 0 */, tail: string /* <= 4096 bytes */, log_path: string, note: string } [amended post-QA-gate: 'spawn_error' added for any non-ENOENT/non-ENOBUFS/non-ETIMEDOUT spawn error, carrying res.error.message in note — see backend-notes.md]

EXPORTS: captureBaseline({cwd, reason}) -> Baseline · readBaseline(path) -> Baseline|null · writeBaselineExclusive(path, baseline) -> void · touchedPaths({cwd, baseline}) -> Touched · parsePorcelainZ(buf) -> PorcelainEntry[] · statusSnapshot(cwd) -> PorcelainEntry[] · gitAnchorSha(cwd) -> string · hashFileSha256(absPath) -> string|null · runValidationCommand({cmd, cwd, timeoutMs, logPath}) -> RunResult · GATE_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS (frozen number) · EVIDENCE_ERROR_CODES (frozen string[]) · class EvidenceError extends Error { code }

EVIDENCE_ERROR_CODES = ['git_unavailable', 'unborn_branch', 'status_unparseable', 'baseline_malformed', 'baseline_exists']
